#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175708 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f10a926-3cfd-5575-ae19-59f95cdca45f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Real Root"}] :question/id #uuid "a6d40e97-c5d4-55e1-8a4c-56be1fc2d731" :question/math-academy-id "e-5794" :question/problem "Given that $x=-2$ is a solution to the equation $x^3+8 = 0,$ find the remaining solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x) = x^3+8.$ Since $x=-2$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/assets/a92f1e322fc91c8388e7969117aca8e616625128d82ee12c88ce108cb6b2637e.png)\n\nThus, $f(x) = (x+2)(x^2-2x+4).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $x^2-2x+4 = 0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+3 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+3 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-3 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-2, \\, 1\\pm \\sqrt3\\textrm{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175734 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dc69a9e-6344-5a92-beb7-0c9fef8171d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Complex Root"}] :question/id #uuid "054cbbc8-22ef-585d-bf8a-849eabdc09a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8800" :question/problem "Find all of the solutions to $x^3-3x^2+12x-10 = 0$ given that $x=1+3\\text{i}$ is a solution." :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x) = x^3-3x^2+12x-10.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and $x_1=1+3\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overline{x_1}=\\overline{1+3\\textrm{i}}=1-3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$\\big(x-(1+3\\text{i})\\big)\\big(x-(1-3\\text{i})\\big)$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(1+3\\text{i}+1-3\\text{i})x+(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+({1}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2 - 2x + 10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-1 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-2x+10\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+12x-10\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}+2x-10 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,-\\,{x}^{2}+2x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x) = (x^2 - 2x + 10)(x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=1, 1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dc69a9e-6344-5a92-beb7-0c9fef8171d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Complex Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89d4640a-7f5c-5644-aa43-88283bdb1419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{1}{4}, 2 ± 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173251} {:answer/id #uuid "95b59b75-0b50-5ba5-acf1-dd4bb3d10abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{1}{8}, 2 ± 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173252} {:answer/id #uuid "a252ec0f-a961-57b8-b0ee-8e902babccce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{1}{2}, 2 ± 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173253} {:answer/id #uuid "b431f70b-c50f-5aaf-8306-53623badba1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = ± \\frac{1}{8}, 2 - 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173254} {:answer/id #uuid "61f3c116-cfed-5743-adc9-63af82f5ef28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{1}{4}, \\frac{1}{2}, 2 - 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a252ec0f-a961-57b8-b0ee-8e902babccce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{1}{2}, 2 ± 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173253} :answer-field/id #uuid "74e9cc28-9979-5c2f-a8b5-e0827d82fb9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1dbcaa6d-a062-5bd6-9123-63e6ea9f493b" :question/math-academy-id "q-101758" :question/problem "Find all of the solutions to $2{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+20x-8=0$ given that $x=2-2\\text{i}$ is a solution." :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+20x-8.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=2-2\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{2-2\\text{i}}=2+2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(2-2\\text{i}))(x-(2+2\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(2-2\\text{i}))(x-(2+2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(2-2\\text{i}+2+2\\text{i})x+(2-2\\text{i})(2+2\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+({2}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+8 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-4x+8$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x-1 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-4x+8\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+20x-8\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+16x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}+4x-8 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,{x}^{2}+4x-8}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+8)(2x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=\\frac{1}{2},2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f10a926-3cfd-5575-ae19-59f95cdca45f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Real Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "455737fa-8f9a-5c53-9c32-0e89354e4b0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350344} {:answer/id #uuid "adde2b37-c2fa-57f9-82a7-11b7925d1c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350345} {:answer/id #uuid "1e51a76e-05c7-5669-bcc7-f5f410503352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350346} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8bee53-4dd6-5937-be20-fb0df3a13e79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350347} {:answer/id #uuid "eeba1ff4-0775-53e9-a7fa-0bfccf059128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "455737fa-8f9a-5c53-9c32-0e89354e4b0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350344} :answer-field/id #uuid "b393ecb0-87e2-5c0d-b32a-76f10eae3e34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbb34119-16b7-5bfa-b1df-ad34b590b96b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104497" :question/problem "Given that $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a solution to the equation $3{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+8x-2=0,$ find the remaining solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=9{x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}+24x-6.$ Since $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x-\\frac{1}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-\\frac{1}{3})$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{\\frac{1}{3}} & 3 & -7 & 8 & -2 \\\\  &  & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\  & 3 & -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{3})(3{x}^{2}-6x+6).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $3{x}^{2}-6x+6=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x+6 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-2x+2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =(\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,1\\pm \\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f10a926-3cfd-5575-ae19-59f95cdca45f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Real Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8efd36e9-47b3-5236-a102-2ed9df14f422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 ± \\frac{1}{9}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173431} {:answer/id #uuid "bcba977f-5856-5530-aebf-21d486e961ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173432} {:answer/id #uuid "14136c98-a7df-5233-a316-60d1c780b49c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± \\frac{1}{3}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173433} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0ca391-e8ca-501b-9377-2735e5aa82ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 ± \\frac{1}{3}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173434} {:answer/id #uuid "4ced3c98-2fd6-5041-b2be-fb275665ba01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 9\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14136c98-a7df-5233-a316-60d1c780b49c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± \\frac{1}{3}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173433} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec82d6ae-8fb8-58ed-8e55-b5f8ab5ae118" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3c1abf5-afe7-52c3-834d-bd3dc3ee2724" :question/math-academy-id "q-104500" :question/problem "Given that $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a solution to the equation $36{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+4x+1=0,$ find the remaining solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=36{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+4x+1.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ using synthetic division.\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $36$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-9$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $36$ | $0$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(36{x}^{2}+4).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $36{x}^{2}+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ 36{x}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dc69a9e-6344-5a92-beb7-0c9fef8171d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Complex Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "543b8056-a152-55ea-b4f0-c37e96cdcda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,3\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350351} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d4803a-4caa-5055-9714-ac773f698278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2,3\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350352} {:answer/id #uuid "deb451b3-1cd7-524c-bd2d-f02d71bae83c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,3-\\text{i},1+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350353} {:answer/id #uuid "0162a096-183a-5bdb-b167-ac7db62e2786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,0,3-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350354} {:answer/id #uuid "a9851d43-c8fa-5651-be51-cf276eb13d4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2,3-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "543b8056-a152-55ea-b4f0-c37e96cdcda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,3\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350351} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f4d0c9a-78d1-525e-87e4-8a5e9d36fa42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f46539e8-9ace-5c88-b4d4-dec07a27569b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104514" :question/problem "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20=0$ given that $x=3-\\text{i}$ is a solution." :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=3-\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{3-\\text{i}}=3+\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(3-\\text{i}))(x-(3+\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(3-\\text{i}))(x-(3+\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(3-\\text{i}+(3+\\text{i)})x+(3-\\text{i})(3+\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+({3}^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-6x+10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x+2 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-6x+10\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{x}^{2}-12x+20 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}-12x+20}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-6x+10)(x+2).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-2,3\\pm \\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dc69a9e-6344-5a92-beb7-0c9fef8171d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Complex Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b75513b2-10b8-518b-9b52-6ef6a78b844c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,-5\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350358} {:answer/id #uuid "f48e3d00-70bb-5aa8-be87-5511459174e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,0,-5+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350359} {:answer/id #uuid "1e228e50-14d0-5013-bb46-64fb20792755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,-5\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350360} {:answer/id #uuid "04b18ffd-5215-509a-9893-39bb39e967d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,4,-5+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350361} {:answer/id #uuid "067119f7-1bfb-5e89-97a4-0a56ea3c3710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 4,-5+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e228e50-14d0-5013-bb46-64fb20792755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,-5\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350360} :answer-field/id #uuid "286b9f8e-928f-5607-a71f-9eb67ce9bff9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "11ab4f0c-a250-5489-90e6-0b0b8a5999d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104516" :question/problem "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11x-116=0$ given that $x=-5+2\\text{i}$ is a solution." :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11x-116.$\n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=-5+2\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{-5+2\\text{i}}=-5-2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(-5+2\\text{i}))(x-(-5-2\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-5+2\\text{i}))(x-(-5-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(-5+2\\text{i}+(-5-2\\text{i)})x+(-5+2\\text{i})(-5-2\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+((-5)^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+29 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+10x+29$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+10x+29\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11x-116} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}+29x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,-4{x}^{2}+40x-116 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{-4{x}^{2}+40x-116}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n        \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+10x+29)(x-4).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=4,-5\\pm 2\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f10a926-3cfd-5575-ae19-59f95cdca45f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Real Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a979f27-5eed-5578-b130-f63c0032ad33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350365} {:answer/id #uuid "00e9ee47-8163-514f-8c70-e64bd59d74d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350366} {:answer/id #uuid "e08c910a-da01-5914-a54f-dd29480ef77d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350367} {:answer/id #uuid "7aacdd41-ec92-529b-a070-c36dff0f9e7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350368} {:answer/id #uuid "c08c54b0-c058-57bf-8046-b30d81e440a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a979f27-5eed-5578-b130-f63c0032ad33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350365} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6c8a277-2073-577b-a7cd-16a08da84faa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3416f819-c147-56ca-a73a-756cccad298c" :question/math-academy-id "q-107545" :question/problem "Given that $x=5$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+29x-45=0,$ find the remaining solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+29x-45.$ Since $x=5$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{5} & 1 & -9 & 29 & -45 \\\\  &  & 5 & -20 & 45 \\\\  & 1 & -4 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x-5)({x}^{2}-4x+9).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}-4x+9=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-5 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=5,\\,2\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dc69a9e-6344-5a92-beb7-0c9fef8171d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Complex Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "414c2ee4-cb13-5e5b-bf64-75d82d3f856f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,2,3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350372} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ae4198-e5e5-5c93-941b-c13b80d3e03a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3,\\pm 3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350373} {:answer/id #uuid "896bae99-bfc5-5d93-8aab-2116eabc6011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2,\\pm 3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350374} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc1a1d8-ca8f-51b0-86f3-04c924a8180c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,\\pm 3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350375} {:answer/id #uuid "75dc36bf-36ec-5d8f-9d42-43dfcc1f2532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,3,3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcc1a1d8-ca8f-51b0-86f3-04c924a8180c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,\\pm 3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350375} :answer-field/id #uuid "50cc86fd-84ec-5f56-a3b1-bab9059e6fce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8957c2b-9ff3-53f8-83c1-0ad5de59519d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107548" :question/problem "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+9x-9=0$ given that $x=-3\\text{i}$ is a solution." :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+9x-9.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=-3\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{-3\\text{i}}=3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-3\\text{i})(x-(-3\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3\\text{i})(x-(-3\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}+(3\\text{i})(-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+9$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & {x}^{2}+9\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+9x-9\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+9x}_{―} \\\\  & -{x}^{2}\\,\\,-9 \\\\  & \\,{-\\,{x}^{2}\\,\\,-9}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+9)(x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=1,\\pm 3\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f10a926-3cfd-5575-ae19-59f95cdca45f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Real Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e56f057f-f61f-51d8-99dd-3a415f8cb38e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350379} {:answer/id #uuid "67942167-2577-583d-8cf6-3042ee870463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pm 4\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350380} {:answer/id #uuid "d95f989f-c2d4-5422-a6b9-7760ecb85bde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 3\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350381} {:answer/id #uuid "abd8a9c2-9326-5f80-8d62-99ce9348339c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 2\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350382} {:answer/id #uuid "8286d7c8-a8e5-5c7f-89b7-3a1aa6d3bdac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 3\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e56f057f-f61f-51d8-99dd-3a415f8cb38e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350379} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1e03f55-9e8e-5d3f-b579-70c85ecbe37c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0cf02442-8d73-58e6-a299-ebbbd1788a53" :question/math-academy-id "q-17409" :question/problem "Given that $x=-10$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-24x+160=0,$ find the remaining solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-24x+160.$ Since $x=-10$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+10)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+10)$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{-10} & 1 & 6 & -24 & 160 \\\\  &  & -10 & 40 & -160 \\\\  & 1 & -4 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+10)({x}^{2}-4x+16).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}-4x+16=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+16 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-12 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =(2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i} \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-10,\\,2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f10a926-3cfd-5575-ae19-59f95cdca45f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Real Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ab9c5a9-5607-5462-9e6a-27fcf3281e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ab9c5a9-5607-5462-9e6a-27fcf3281e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350386} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7706a19-8962-5c0e-ab22-27413dfcd17e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "befb63f6-09c7-53f3-a3d5-95104472d1e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-317978" :question/problem "Given that $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a solution to the equation $4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+100x+25=0,$ find the remaining solutions.\n\n$x=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+100x+25.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ using synthetic division.\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $4$ | $1$ | $100$ | $25$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-25$ |\n|  | $4$ | $0$ | $100$ | $0$ |\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}+100).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $4{x}^{2}+100=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =-100 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(5\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm 5\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dc69a9e-6344-5a92-beb7-0c9fef8171d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Complex Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ffa7934-b256-534d-9750-eabde4b512bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ffa7934-b256-534d-9750-eabde4b512bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350389} :answer-field/id #uuid "749dd247-28ca-5b74-9f06-5989bc4372ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350388} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb7929e8-4ee8-5377-b930-314c1224be2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb7929e8-4ee8-5377-b930-314c1224be2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350391} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c308244-90cf-530d-8c47-5d6bf7eb1e77" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "177f798f-98fb-53b0-bae4-ba0d79693695" :question/math-academy-id "q-317986" :question/problem "Determine all the solutions to ${x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+25x-25=0$ given that $x=5\\text{i}$ is a solution. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=\\pm $\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+25x-25.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=5\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{5\\text{i}}=-5\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, $(x-5\\text{i})(x-(-5\\text{i}))$ is a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5\\text{i})(x-(-5\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}+(5\\text{i})(-5\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+25$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & {x}^{2}+25\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+25x-25\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+25x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}\\,-25 \\\\  & \\,\\,{\\,-{x}^{2}\\,\\,-25}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+25)(x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=1$ and $x=\\pm \\,5\\text{i}.$"}]}
